\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa300\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs36\b\cf1 mohdEduApp - Lecturer Q&A and Rubric Answers\par
\fs22\i\cf2 Complete assessment presentation & rubric answer guide for Lecturer evaluation.\par
\b0\i0\sa200\sl276\slmult1
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Are there any formatting or lint warnings?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: No, the code adheres strictly to the official Kotlin style guide with zero compiler or linter warnings.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Explain your naming conventions and comments.\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We use clear, self-documenting PascalCase for classes (Course, HomeScreen, QuizActivityScreen, ProgressSyncWorker) and camelCase for properties and functions, supported by clean architectural comments.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How is your project structured into packages?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We organize code into clear sub-packages (ui.theme, worker, etc.) following clean architecture separation of concerns.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Does the app have levels or categories?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Yes! The app features course categories (All, Mobile, Algorithms, Architecture, Database) allowing students to filter courses by difficulty and topic.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How does the random feature work?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: As noted in our quiz module, when a student opens a course quiz, the app maintains a pool of 10 questions and uses Kotlin's .shuffled().take(3) to dynamically display 3 random questions every time the quiz is launched.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Does the app incorporate sound?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Yes, we integrate Android's ToneGenerator to provide subtle audio feedback (TONE_PROP_ACK for correct answers and TONE_PROP_NACK for incorrect answers during quizzes).\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: What happens if I rotate the screen during a quiz or search?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Rotation is fully handled. All UI state, search queries, enrolled courses, and active quiz question indexes use Compose remember and state hoisting so state is preserved across configuration changes.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Where are scores tracked?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Quiz results are automatically recorded into a Quiz Score Record List displayed in the My Learning tab, showing past scores, dates, and course titles.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Does the app have a logo and cohesive color theme?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Yes, the app features a customized adaptive launcher icon and a Material 3 dynamic color palette that seamlessly coordinates between light and dark modes.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How do you handle scrolling and layout spacing?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: All screens use unified LazyColumn layouts with edge-to-edge padding (enableEdgeToEdge), ensuring smooth scrolling and zero clipping under system bars.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How do you handle safety and user actions?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: 1. Delete Confirmation: When removing an enrolled course from 'My Learning', an explicit AlertDialog confirmation dialog appears ('Are you sure you want to remove this course?'). 2. Delete vs Edit: Deleting removes course enrollment while editing/settings allow adjusting preferences (dark mode, notifications). 3. Input Validation: Search inputs are validated in real-time against course titles and descriptions.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How is navigation implemented?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We use a modern Material 3 Bottom Navigation Bar (NavigationBar / NavigationBarItem) with three primary tabs (Home, My Learning & Scores, Settings), plus drill-down navigation into Course Details and Quiz Activity.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: What architecture pattern did you use?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We use MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) with Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF) and the Repository pattern to decouple UI from business logic.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How do you persist data?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We use Preferences DataStore for persistent local storage of user enrollment, progress, and preferences.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: How is networking simulated?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Asynchronous data fetching is implemented using suspending Coroutine functions simulating remote server calls.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Explain your background tasks and permissions.\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: WorkManager: ProgressSyncWorker handles periodic background synchronization of study progress. Runtime Permissions: Push notifications (POST_NOTIFICATIONS) include an ethical pre-prompt rationale dialog explaining study reminder benefits in plain language before showing the system prompt, fully respecting user denial.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: What unit tests did you write?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: We wrote non-GUI unit tests (CourseModelTest.kt) verifying model integrity, course enrollment states, and course filtering logic. All unit tests pass successfully (3/3 tests passed).\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: Show me your GitHub repo and README.\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Our GitHub repository features frequent, meaningful commit messages and a comprehensive README.md documenting architecture, advanced APIs, and ethical design.\par}\sa300\par
{\b\fs26\cf1 Q: What did you reflect upon?\par}
{\fs22\cf2 Answer: Our self-reflection applies Gibbs' Reflective Cycle to our Assessment 2 research on ACS Professional Ethics, privacy-by-design, data minimization, and user autonomy.\par}\sa300\par
